  "chain": "insulating stab covers left on bus stabs [cause A] -> breaker 2BKR52-2411 arc-flash fault -> upstream feeder breaker 2BKR52-2412 trips -> loss of power to 24A bus 2BUS2B04A -> loss of 2PUMPFW21 (low suction) & CEDS de-energized -> lowering SG levels -> manual reactor trip -> valid AFAS actuation [backup AFW restores SG levels]",
  "golden_questions": [
    "Q1",
    "Q3",
    "Q4",
    "Q14"
  ],
  "notes": "Two parallel downstream effects from bus loss modeled separately (fw21 trip and CEDS de-energization) per rule 5. 2PUMPFW22's post-trip high-discharge-pressure trip/speed-control failure is modeled as a separate FailureMode feeding the same consequence, since the narrative treats it as a distinct 'did not operate as designed' finding rather than a direct product of the arc-flash chain, though it occurred amid the same transient. Manufacturer code W120 mapped generically to Westinghouse per typical NRC manufacturer coding; not explicitly named in narrative text. AFW modeled as backup system per explicit statement that AFW pumps restored SG levels (operable during event). CEDS and AFAS treated as involved systems (not design-context) since they actively participated in the failure/consequence chain."
